/* Every Linux x86-64 syscall, and the answer this kernel gives it.
 *
 *   SYSCALL(number, name, state, phase, errno, "why")
 *
 * The single statement of each number (docs/abi/ invariant 6). The kernel reads
 * it (kernel/abi/abi_linux.c) to answer a number that has no row; the scripts
 * read it to check every row (check-syscall-checks.py) and to write
 * docs/abi/syscalls.md (make-syscall-table.py). Three statements of one number
 * that could disagree are now one statement and two checks.
 *
 * state   DONE      a row serves it, with Linux's semantics as far as tested
 *         PARTIAL   a row serves it with a gap; "why" names the gap, and the
 *                   phase is the one that closes it
 *         MISSING   no row yet; the phase of docs/abi/phases.md that owns it.
 *                   Reaching one is a failure the boot gate reports by number.
 *         DEFERRED  no row, and none planned until a program asks; ENOSYS,
 *                   counted apart - the gate reports one being reached.
 *         REFUSED   no row, by decision; "errno" is the answer and "why" the
 *                   reason. Expected, counted, never a failure.
 * phase   NONE (done), NATIVE (VibeOS's own, 1000 and up), L1-L9, D, R
 * errno   the Linux errno a row-less number returns: ENOSYS or EPERM; NONE when
 *         a row answers
 *
 * Seeded on 2026-09-29 from Linux's asm/unistd_64.h (0-334 and 424-461) and the
 * plan's table; edited by hand from here on. Adding a row means changing its
 * line here to DONE or PARTIAL in the same change - the boot refuses a row whose
 * line says otherwise, and so does check-syscall-checks.py.
 */
SYSCALL(  0, read,                    DONE,     NONE,  NONE,   "")
SYSCALL(  1, write,                   DONE,     NONE,  NONE,   "")
SYSCALL(  2, open,                    DONE,     NONE,  NONE,   "")
